Governor limits are a set of limits in Apex that control the amount of system resources that can be consumed by a single transaction. These limits help ensure that the performance of the overall Salesforce platform is not impacted by a single resource-intensive transaction.

Here's an example of how governor limits can affect the behavior of an Apex class:
```csharp
public class MyClass {
    public static void doSomething() {
        List<Account> accounts = [SELECT Name FROM Account LIMIT 50000];
        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            acc.Name = acc.Name + ' Updated';
            update acc;
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `doSomething` method retrieves a list of up to 50,000 `Account` records and updates the `Name` field of each record by appending the text `' Updated'`. However, this code will exceed the governor limit for the number of records that can be processed in a single transaction, which is typically around 10,000 records.

As a result, when this code is executed, a `System.LimitException` will be thrown and the transaction will be terminated. To avoid this issue, the code could be modified to process the records in smaller batches, or to use an asynchronous process (such as a batch class) to update the records in a separate transaction.

By understanding and working within the governor limits, you can ensure that your Apex code runs efficiently and reliably, and that it does not impact the performance of the overall Salesforce platform.

