Here is an example of how you might use std::future and std::async to fetch the data in a separate thread:
```
std::future<int> data_future = std::async(std::launch::async, [](){
    // fetch data here
    return /* ... */;
});
```
This will fetch the data in a separate thread and return a future that can be used to access the result of the fetch operation at a later time.

You can use the `.get()` method on the future object to wait for the result of the async operation and retrieve the value, or use the `.wait()` method to check if the async operation has finished.
```
int data = data_future.get();
```
It's also good to note that you can use `std::shared_future<T>` instead of `std::future<T>` if you want multiple threads to access the same shared state.
